INT. HOSPICE WING - NIGHT SHIFT NURSES' STATION

Low light. Quiet monitors. A call button blinks red on a wall panel. Somewhere down the hall, a television murmurs a game show to nobody.

GRACELYN "GRACE" ADEYEMI, fifties, charge nurse, signs a chart and doesn't look at her visitor. DEVON MARSH, thirties, the patient's son, stands at the counter in a rumpled suit, a paper cup of cold coffee in his hand.

INTERCOM (V.O.)
Station, room fourteen. Mr. Marsh is asking for his son again.

GRACE
(into the panel)
On my way, Doreen.

She lifts a finger to Devon. He doesn't move.

DEVON
He keeps calling me Walter.

GRACE
That's his brother. Passed in '98.

DEVON
I know who Walter is.

GRACE
Then you know it's not an insult.

Devon puts down the cup. His hand shakes slightly.

DEVON
Grace, I'm not ready for this.

GRACE
Nobody is, Devon. We just show up.

INTERCOM (V.O.)
(softer)
Grace. He's asking for the window open.

GRACE
(into the panel)
Open it, Doreen. Let him have the air.

She turns back to Devon.

GRACE (CONT'D)
Go. Say you're Walter if you have to.

DEVON
He'd know.

GRACE
He'd know and he'd want it anyway.

A long breath. Devon straightens his tie, then loosens it again.

DEVON
You've done this a lot.

GRACE
Thirty-one years.

DEVON
Does it get easier?

GRACE
(gently)
No. You get kinder.

He nods. He goes. She watches him go down the hall until the dark takes his shoulders.

DOREEN (O.S.)
Grace? He's saying hello to somebody.

GRACE
(to herself)
Good.

LEONIDAS MARSH (O.S.)
(faint, delighted)
Walter? Is that you, Walter?

Grace closes her eyes a moment. She picks up the pen and writes the time.
